* t35cent.a -- CONF68K test t35: subtracting two Julian dates gives the* number of days between them, across a century year that is NOT a leap year.** Claim (DOCS/claims.md): "To compute the number of days between two dates,* subtract the lower Julian date number from the higher number"* (Technical Manual, F$Julian). The calendar is Gregorian -- the same page* fixes the reform at October 15, 1582 -- so February 1900 has 28 days:* 1900 is divisible by 100 but not by 400, so it is NOT a leap year. This* is the case the plain every-four-years rule gets wrong; t34 is its* companion, where 2000 IS a leap year because 400 divides it.** This is worth a test because it cannot be checked by a round trip. The* corpus test this was promoted from (test/68k-live-verification/* batch9-01.a) converts a date and converts it back, which reproduces the* input bit-for-bit no matter what epoch or calendar the implementation* uses -- its own header says so. A DIFFERENCE has an answer the manual* fixes independently of the epoch, so it can disagree with the system.** An implementation using the plain every-4-years rule passes t34 and* FAILS this one, reporting 29. That is the whole reason both exist.** Date is packed (year:16)(month:8)(day:8) -- the manual writes that as* "yyyymmdd", meaning the field layout and not decimal digits.*Prgrm set 1Objct set 1ReEnt set $80Type_Lang set (Prgrm<<8)+ObjctAttr_Rev set (ReEnt<<8)+1IWrite set $8AFExit set $06FJulian set $20*EXPECT set 28* psect t35cent,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,0,2048,startstart moveq #0,d0 move.l #$076C0201,d1 trap #0 dc.w FJulian bcs.w setuperr move.l d1,d5* moveq #0,d0 move.l #$076C0301,d1 trap #0 dc.w FJulian bcs.w setuperr sub.l d5,d1 moveq #0,d6 move.l d1,d6 cmpi.l #EXPECT,d6 beq.w ispass bra.w isfail* use verdict.i use report.i*idstr dc.b "RESULT t35 ",0passtr dc.b "PASS",0failstr dc.b "FAIL",0errstr dc.b "ERROR",0obsstr dc.b "  obs=",0expstr dc.b " exp=",0descstr dc.b "  Julian difference counts days: February 1900 has 28",0 ends